            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the German for the guarantee, then the German for to guarantee."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: klappen",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "klappen — \"to work out (informal); to fold\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mit der neuen Wohnung hat es endlich geklappt. It finally worked out with the new flat."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Informal and very common: Das klappt nicht — that does not work. Literally to fold or flap: den Sitz nach vorne klappen."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "klappen imitates a sharp sound; EtymDB lists English clap and Dutch klappen as cousins. It is usually said to have come into the standard language from Low German. The sense \"to work out\" is usually explained as things snapping neatly into place."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
